{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "223",
    "case_summary": "A 67-year-old woman with a 4-day history of fever and cough was admitted to a local hospital, where SARS-CoV-2 infection was confirmed by nasopharyngeal PCR. Five days after admission, her respiratory status worsened, prompting intubation and transfer to the ICU. Her comorbidities included diabetes mellitus with dialysis-dependent nephropathy, angina, prior gastric cancer resection, and spinal‐canal stenosis; she was on an antiplatelet agent.  \nOn arrival: temperature 34 °C; blood pressure 110/65 mmHg; heart rate 102 beats/min; GCS E1VtM1 under deep sedation. Arterial blood gas (on pressure‐control ventilation: PEEP 8 cmH₂O, PiP 25 cmH₂O, FiO₂ 0.5) showed pH 7.309, PaCO₂ 41.5 mmHg, PaO₂ 78.2 mmHg.  \nLaboratory studies: WBC 13,900/µL; CRP 13.1 mg/dL; D-dimer 7.25 µg/mL; APTT 170 s; INR 1.35.  \nChest CT: bilateral ground‐glass opacities with lower-lung predominance. Abdominal CT: no overt abnormalities or thromboembolism, but diffuse edema of the intestine, including the transverse colon, and marked sclerotic changes in abdominal vessels.  \nShe continued dexamethasone (6 mg/day) and commenced continuous renal replacement therapy. On ICU day 3, cefepime was started for ventilator-associated pneumonia. The APTT fell to 51.5 s, and prophylactic-dose unfractionated heparin was initiated to target an APTT of ~60 s.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. COVID-19-related coagulopathy\nB. of having been induced by COVID\nC. Colonic pseudo\nD. Interleukin\nE. Nonocclusive mesenteric ischemia was considered given the critical illness context, but histopathology did not match\nF. COVID\nG. None\nH. None\n",
